// Where a sentence ends — UAX #29 sentence boundaries.
//
// Splitting on `.` gets "Dr. Smith" wrong, "3.14" wrong, "etc." wrong and
// "..." wrong, in that order of how often it happens. A summariser, a
// screen reader, a translation memory and a "read time" estimate all need
// the same answer, and the rules that give it are not "a full stop
// followed by a space".
//
// ## The default is *not* to break
//
// Every other segmentation in UAX #29 breaks unless a rule says otherwise.
// This one continues unless a rule says otherwise, because most characters
// are in the middle of a sentence. So the rules describe the endings, and
// everything else is the middle.
//
// ## A full stop is only an ending in company
//
// `ATerm` — the ordinary full stop — ends a sentence only when what
// follows is not a lower-case letter, not a digit, and not one of the
// characters that continue a sentence. `3.14` continues because a digit
// follows; `Mr. Smith` does *not* end, because the rule looks past the
// space to a capital preceded by a capital-then-period, which is the
// abbreviation shape. `He said "no." Then left.` does end, and the closing
// quote and the space are part of the first sentence.
//
// ## What this is not
//
// An abbreviation dictionary. UAX #29 is deliberately language-neutral and
// gets `Dr. Smith` wrong on purpose — no rule can know that `Dr` is an
// abbreviation and `Smith` is not without a list. A caller that needs that
// tailors on top; what is here is the part that is the same everywhere.

/// Triples: first code point, last, and the Sentence_Break class.
